<title>UK: UK government wins key vote after budget debate.</title>
<headline>UK government wins key vote after budget debate.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-12-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>British Prime Minister John Major's government had a parliamentary majority of five on Tuesday in defeating a motion by the main opposition Labour party to reduce value added tax on domestic fuel to five percent from eight percent.</p>
<p>The government won the vote, the fist of a series, in the 651-seat House of Commons by 317 votes to 312.</p>
<p>Labour had not expected to win approval for any of the amendments it proposed to Chancellor of the Exchequer Kenneth Clarke's annual budget, presented last Tuesday.</p>
<p>-- London newsroom 44-171-542 7767</p>
